dirk NEWS — history of user-visible changes.

Copyright (C) 2026 Oddur Sigurdsson.
See the end of the file for copying conditions.

Please send bug reports and suggestions through the project's issue tracker.


* Noteworthy changes in release 0.5.0 (2026-09-08)

Four milestones of work, and the theme running through them is that dirk
stopped being a thing you look at and became a thing you can drive.  A session
can now be read, searched, rearranged and copied out of; started, prompted and
waited on from a script; and watched from two terminals at once.

** Reading what has gone past

Scrollback, at last.  The wheel and the keys scroll the focused pane, the rail
says how far back you are, and copy mode selects by character, word, line and
paragraph with the pointer or without it.  What you select goes to the system
clipboard through OSC 52, so it works over ssh.

`/` searches the pane you are reading, incrementally, with the matches
highlighted and next and previous bound.  Leaving a search puts you back where
you were rather than at the bottom, because a search you abandoned should not
cost you your place.  A wider search reads every pane in the session and names
the pane each hit came from.

** Tabs, and moving panes between them

A workspace holds tabs; a tab holds a split tree.  An editor and a test runner
can live in one space without being on screen together, and `pane move` sends a
pane to another tab.  Tabs are named by hand and by the naming policy, like
everything else with a name in dirk.

A pane can be zoomed to fill its workspace and put back, and two panes can be
swapped without closing either.  Splits resize from the keyboard as well as by
dragging the edge between them.

** Two terminals, one session

A second client no longer takes the session over.  Each gets its own view: its
own focus, its own sidebar state, its own idea of what is on screen.  The
session is one thing and looking at it is not.

** Driving a session from outside

The socket API grew the half that acts rather than reports.  `pane run` submits
a command, `pane send-text` and `pane send-keys` type into a pane, and `pane
wait-output` blocks until something appears rather than sleeping and hoping.
`agent prompt` gives an agent work; `agent wait` blocks until it reaches a
state.  Waits are answered on the turn the state actually changed, so a wait
for `blocked` ends when the agent blocks and not on the next poll.

`pane attach` puts one pane in your terminal with no interface around it, which
is what a script driving a single program wants.  `dirk api schema` prints the
whole surface as data, `dirk session notify` raises an interruption from a
script, and there are completions for bash, zsh and fish generated from the
same table so they cannot describe a command that no longer exists.

** Agents you can start, and that tell you what they are doing

`agent start` launches one rather than merely recognising it, and reports
`starting` for a pane whose process has not appeared in the table yet — which
is exactly the moment the state is most worth having.  `n` from the nav starts
an agent rather than a shell, because a workspace with a shell in it has no
intent and never gets a name.

Harnesses are configuration now, not a table compiled into the program: a row
in `config.toml` adds one, and the shipped rules are files you can replace one
at a time.  `wrappers` tells dirk what is behind `uv run` or `poetry run`.  An
agent can report its own state through a hook rather than being inferred at,
and `agent explain` says which signal decided the state it is in and why — the
same pass that decided it, not a second account of it.

`agent hooks install` edits the harness's own settings file instead of printing
something to paste.  Conversations resume where they were rather than merely in
the right directory, and a full-screen agent's history can be read out of a
pane that redraws over itself.

** The sidebar at forty projects

The tree was right at five workspaces and wrong at forty.  Rows are one line
now, with `[nav] rows` choosing whether that line leads with what a workspace
*is* — its branch — or with what it is *doing*, which is the arrangement for
somebody whose projects are one checkout each.  A collapsed project still says
what is inside it.

The rail was rebuilt around the same question.  Its first cells say whose
account, which session and which machine, rather than repeating the name of the
program you just started.  Four levels of emphasis replaced one flat dim.  What
it gives up as the terminal narrows is a ladder now — the clock goes first,
then the pane counter, then the project — rather than whatever happened to be
measured last, so it can no longer keep the clock and drop the count of agents
waiting for a human.  The corner belongs to detach, which is the safe thing you
do several times a day; quit is not something the pointer can land on by
overshooting.

Elision keeps the end of a label rather than the start, because the end is
where the distinguishing word usually is.

** Attention, and hearing about it

The permanent agents section is gone; what is owed appears when something is
owed and takes no room when nothing is.  There is a sound when an agent blocks
and a different one when it is done — the two events worth hearing, since
working and idle are conditions rather than news.  Per-agent rules turn either
off for a particular harness.

Boards report without being opened: a layout row can run a command and wear its
answer as a badge, which is the difference between a link called "git" and a
row that says the tree is dirty and two commits behind.

** Worktrees, from the nav

Making a worktree used to mean leaving dirk, running `git worktree add`, coming
back and opening the project.  It is one action now, from the project row or
over the socket, and it leaves you in a workspace on the new branch.

** Images in a pane

A program that draws a picture — a plot, a diagram, a screenshot an agent just
took — used to have it vanish the moment it was inside dirk.  Kitty graphics
now pass through: sent once and placed many times, anchored to a line of the
pane's history so they scroll with the text they belong to, clipped to their
pane, and not redrawn when nothing about them has changed.  A terminal that
cannot draw one ignores what dirk sends, so there is nothing to configure;
`[terminal] graphics = false` is for a terminal that does something worse than
ignore it.

** The terminal dirk is running in

dirk sets its window title from `[ui] window_title`, so a tabbed terminal says
which session, machine and workspace each tab holds.  `[terminal] shell_mode`
decides whether a new pane gets a login shell, `[terminal] new_cwd` decides
where it starts, and `[ui] mouse = false` gives the terminal's own selection
back to anyone who wants it.  Panes can carry a rule across the top with a
label, which is what tells two shells side by side apart.

** Keys

Everything bindable from `[keys]`, with or without the prefix: a chord like
`ctrl+alt+d` reaches an action directly, and a binding can have both forms.
dirk complains about a binding that will never arrive — a chord the terminal
takes first, or one a shell has already spent — rather than accepting it and
doing nothing.  The command palette lists every action, filtered by typing, so
finding out what dirk can do no longer means reading its source.

** Fixes

A CLI answer through a closed pipe used to end in a backtrace.  `dirk session
list | head -1` now ends the way every other program on the system ends.

`dirk session prune` removes sockets nothing is listening on, which used to
accumulate until the list was mostly things you could not attach to.

A session test that failed used to leave its session running; a red run left
one daemon per failed test on the machine.

Glyphs come from one table with their cell widths declared, so a terminal that
renders them double-width no longer shifts everything to their right, and
`[nav] glyphs = "ascii"` runs dirk somewhere none of them render.

A session with nobody attached has a size that is configuration rather than a
guess, so panes made by a script are not built for a placeholder.


* Noteworthy changes in release 0.4.0 (2026-09-07)

Sessions.  Until now dirk was a program you ran in a terminal, which meant
closing the terminal closed the work.  It is now a session you attach to.

** A session outlives its terminal

`dirk` attaches, starting the session if it is not running; `dirk --no-session`
is what dirk used to be.  Close the terminal and everything in it keeps going.

The server renders and the client paints bytes it does not read.  Shipping state
and letting each client compose it sounds more principled and costs more: two
renderers drift, the local one gets a fix, the remote one does not, and the
difference is a rendering bug nobody can reproduce.  One client at a time; a
second dirk takes the session over and the first is told why.

** Named sessions

`--session NAME` picks one, and `dirk session list` says which exist and whether
anyone is watching.  It answers by connecting rather than by reading the socket
directory: a socket outliving its server is the ordinary state after a crash,
and a list of those is a list of things you cannot attach to.

** A session answers for itself

`dirk pane list`, `pane split`, `pane send-keys`, `workspace rename`, `agent
list`, and the rest.  Answers are JSON, including the failures, because a caller
is a program and prose on stderr is not something a program can branch on.  Ids
are opaque and stable: `w7` is a workspace and `w7:p12` a pane in it, and every
managed pane is told its own, so `--current` works from inside one without
looking anything up.

`dirk --skill` prints what an agent needs in order to drive a session, generated
from the command table so it cannot describe a surface that no longer exists.
Most of it is traps -- that reading does not mark an agent seen, that a
workspace holding two panes stops being renamed -- and each is something that
would otherwise be found out by getting it wrong.

** Reloading

`dirk session reload` re-reads the configuration without restarting.  An open
layout keeps its panes: rebuilding a running dashboard because a colour changed
elsewhere in the file is not a reload, it is a restart.  A file that does not
parse is reported with the running configuration kept, and whatever else was
wrong with it comes back in the answer rather than going to a stderr nobody is
reading.

** Sessions come back

What is written down is the arrangement -- projects, workspaces and their names,
which projects were open -- and deliberately not the screen.  A screenful of text
with nothing behind it is worse than an empty pane, because it looks like
something you can type into.

A name you wrote by hand comes back held, so naming leaves it alone.  A project
whose directory has gone is dropped; one whose volume is merely not mounted is
kept, because those come back.  Writes are atomic and durable, and a saved
session this dirk cannot read is left alone rather than replaced -- it may be
the only record of that arrangement.

** Over ssh

`dirk --remote host` attaches to a session on another machine.  ssh is the whole
authentication story: dirk runs `ssh -T host dirk relay` and drives the ordinary
client over the pipes, and the far side is a program that copies bytes between a
socket and its own stdin and stdout.

A dropped link is not a lost session.  The client keeps the terminal, says so,
and spends two minutes trying to make another, waiting longer between each.  A
link that comes back and dies again without ever painting does not reset that
patience, which is what stops a host that has gone for good from being dialled
once a second for ever.  Keys pressed while there is nowhere to send them are
dropped rather than replayed: they belonged to the screen that was there when
you pressed them.  Ctrl-C leaves while it waits.

** Answers through a pipe

`dirk session list | head -1` used to end in a backtrace.  Every CLI answer went
through `print!`, which panics on a closed pipe rather than returning the error,
so piping one into anything that stops reading -- `head`, a pager you quit, a
`grep -q` -- ended where every other program on the system ends in silence.


* Noteworthy changes in release 0.3.1 (2026-09-07)

The rest of namesync, natively.  The port took its policy -- when a name should
change -- and left everything around it.

** Tokens

A name is made of eleven of them: project, branch, worktree, n, intent,
intent-slug, since, age, locked, stale, agent and agents.  They are an interface
rather than whatever the code has to hand, and a token that is not known is
absent rather than empty, so a template written for a workspace that has a
branch does not leave a hole in one that has not.

Two are clocks, and confusing them makes both useless.  `since` restarts
whenever the state changes and answers "who has been blocked longest".  `age`
restarts only when the intent changes and answers "what has been grinding on the
same thing all day".

** Templates

A name is a template over those tokens, one for workspaces and one for agents,
defaulting to what the labels were before.  A template naming a token that does
not exist is reported at startup rather than rendering as silence.

** Configuration

The similarity threshold, whether hand-written names are respected, whether to
hold off while an agent is blocked, whether to strip a project prefix, and the
list of titles that are programs rather than intents.  That last is what stops a
pane running `nvim` becoming a workspace called `nvim`; it ships with a list.

** Holds

A name you wrote is held explicitly rather than worked out afresh each pass, so
it can be marked in the interface and released with `u`.  Clearing a name by hand
releases the hold: an empty label is not a name, it is the clearest possible
statement that the previous one was unwanted, and a hold with no visible release
leaves a workspace blank for ever.

** A second source

Naming reads the agent's own terminal title.  That is the right primary source
and it fails in one way: a pane where nothing publishes a title has no intent at
all.  `[naming.sources.llm]` covers that case and is off unless asked for.

The model is a source, not a decider.  It supplies a candidate intent from the
tail of the screen, and that candidate goes through precisely the policy every
title goes through.  Nothing about when a name changes moves into the model.

Asked only about panes with no usable title, no more often than a floor, and
never on the drawing thread.  Every failure -- no key, no curl, a timeout, a
malformed answer -- leaves naming exactly where it was without it.  The key is
read from the environment and passed to curl on its standard input, never in an
argument: dirk reads the process table itself and draws what it finds.

** Stale

An agent that has not revised its title across several state changes is marked,
and nothing is done about it.  It is a signal for a human.


* Noteworthy changes in release 0.3.0 (2026-09-06)

** What is running in a pane

dirk reads the tty's foreground process group, which is exactly what is running
in a pane right now -- the thing a shell sets before it waits for a command and
resets when the command ends.  A pane holds a recognised agent, or is a shell at
its prompt, or is running something else that is neither and is named rather
than guessed at.

An agent run under an interpreter is recognised too.  Installed through npm it
executes as `node`, so its executable says nothing and its identity appears only
in its command line; that is the one case where a command line is read at all.

** Four states

  working   producing output
  blocked   an approval prompt is on screen
  done      stopped, and not looked at since
  idle      stopped, and seen

`done` and `idle` are the same underlying state, and what separates them is
whether you have looked.  That is the whole reason `done` exists: finished work
nobody has noticed is the thing worth showing.  A workspace is seen while it is
focused, and unseen for any moment of work you are not watching.

An agent that is thinking redraws continuously, so silence is what says it has
stopped.  Blocked is read from the rows around the cursor, where a question is
being asked, and matched against markers per agent kind -- a phrase alone is not
enough, since agents write "would you like" in ordinary prose, so a marker
counts only above a menu of numbered answers.

** Attention

The rail carries what is owed, and only when it is not zero.  Clicking a count
goes to the workspace that has been in that state longest.  The agents section of
the nav is ordered the same way.

A notification is sent when an agent blocks and when it finishes unseen, never
for the workspace you are looking at, and at most one per workspace per state
per interval.  Configurable, including off.

** Names

Every agent gets one, taken from the intent of the workspace it is in and unique
among live agents.  It follows the agent and is cleared when the pane stops
holding one.  Nothing addresses an agent by name yet; that is v0.4.

** Naming

A blocked agent is no longer renamed after the question it is asking.  Its title
describes the dialog rather than the work, and the label would stick until the
next intent arrived.

** Known limitations

Sessions still do not survive the terminal closing, which is v0.4 entire.  There
is no scrollback view or copy mode, and keybindings are not configurable.  A
notification cannot yet hand a click back to dirk, which is a packaging question.


* Noteworthy changes in release 0.2.0 (2026-09-06)

** The nav

The sidebar is three lists: layouts, spaces and agents.  The order is the
argument -- layouts are places you go, spaces are where work lives, and agents
are what is asking for you, so attention flows down the column.

A workspace row is two lines.  The first is identity: state, number, name, and
how long since it last said anything.  The second is which checkout it is,
marked when that checkout is a worktree.  A workspace with nothing for its
second line draws one line rather than a blank one.

The same workspace appears under spaces and under agents.  That is not
duplication: spaces answers "what is open, and where" and agents answers "what
needs me", and they are sorted differently for that reason.  The agents list is
ordered by what is owed -- blocked, then finished-and-unseen, then working, then
idle, oldest first inside each -- and the numbers stay attached to the
workspace, so a jump key does not move when the order does.

Each section is allocated height before anything is drawn, so a long list of
spaces cannot push the agents section off the bottom.  A section that fits is
never scrolled.  The selection is distinct from focus and the nav takes keys of
its own while it holds them.  The divider drags.

** Layouts

A layout is a named arrangement of programs, declared in the configuration file
as a tree of panes with sizes, and opened as a space of its own.  A layout with
one program and a five-pane dashboard differ only in how many leaves they have.

A pane a layout declared carries a label, drawn as a rule above it.  When its
program exits the pane keeps its rectangle and its last output, says how it
ended on that rule, and `r` starts it again in the same slot.  The output is
usually the point: a board that prints and exits is a reasonable panel.

Panes run in the directory of whatever was focused when the layout opened, and
closing a layout returns you exactly where you were.

A layout whose programs are not all installed is dropped at startup.  It is all
or nothing, because half a dashboard is not a dashboard.

** Panes

A workspace holds a tree of splits rather than a row of equal panes, so nested
arrangements are expressible and a split in a direction the parent already runs
in keeps the shares equal.  Closing a pane gives its space to its own siblings
and moves nothing else.

** Chrome

Section headings are words rather than letter-spaced capitals.  The bar carries
a quit button at its right, which takes two clicks: it ends every shell and
every agent in the session.

** Known limitations

Unchanged from 0.1.0, less the ones above: sessions still do not survive the
terminal closing, there is no scrollback view or copy mode, keybindings are not
configurable, and the state glyph beside a workspace is still inferred from
whether its pane has published a title rather than read from the agent -- so an
agent waiting on a human is not distinguished from one that is working.  That
last is the whole of v0.3.


* Noteworthy changes in release 0.1.0 (2026-09-06)

This is the first release.  It runs as a single process: closing the terminal
ends the session, and detaching is not implemented.  See "Known limitations".

** Panes

Each pane is a process on a pseudo-terminal whose output is parsed into a
terminal grid and painted into the display.  Colours, bold, italic, underline,
inverse and double-width characters are carried through.  Resizing propagates
both to the kernel and to the parser, so programs inside see the size they are
actually given.

Mouse events reach the program in a pane when that program has asked for them,
and are otherwise interpreted by dirk.  A click means "focus this pane" in a
shell and "click this line" in a program that reports mouse input; there is no
mode to switch between the two.

** The sidebar

Static pages sit above the project tree.  A page is a singleton with no project
— one system monitor, one repository browser — spawned when first opened rather
than kept running in the background.  A page whose program is not on PATH is
dropped at startup rather than failing when it is opened.

Below the pages, a project appears once it has a workspace and disappears when
its last one closes.  Every row is a click target.

** Naming

Workspaces are named from the terminal title the program inside publishes, which
is how coding agents report what they are working on.  No model is called and no
credentials are required.

A label dirk did not write is never overwritten.  Placeholder labels are
adopted.  A reworded title describing the same work is not treated as new
intent, a title must hold still before it is committed, and one workspace is
renamed at most once per interval.  Shell prompts, bare paths, echoed command
lines and the plain repository name are never used as names.

** Configuration

dirk runs with no configuration file.  ~/.config/dirk/config.toml overrides
what it names and leaves the rest alone: the brand shown in the bar, where the
project picker looks, the sidebar width, the scrollback depth, the shell, the
list of pages, and the naming thresholds.

** Known limitations

Sessions do not survive the terminal closing.  There is no scrollback view and
no copy mode.  Splits divide a workspace into equal columns or equal rows and
do not nest.  The state glyph beside a workspace is inferred from whether the
pane has ever published a title, so an agent waiting on a human is not
distinguished from one that is working.  Keybindings are not configurable.

Each of these is recorded in the roadmap; see ROADMAP.md.

